# Docker build context of the public repository.
#
# The Dockerfiles build from the ROOT (`context: .`) and copy only `chain/`, `services/` and
# `docker/`. Everything else is dead weight shipped to the daemon on every build -- and the chain
# state of an already-running node can weigh gigabytes.
#
# NEVER exclude `chain/`, `services/` or `docker/`: they are the only sources of the `COPY` steps.
.git
.github
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/*.pyo
**/*.log
*.bak

# Runtime-produced state: never a build input, always bulky.
chain-data/
node_data/
ollama-models/
tokenomics/*.json
tokenomics/*.log

# Documentation and web wallet: served as-is, never copied into an image.
docs/
wallet/

# Build-integrity marker of the mirror: it describes the manufacturing, not the product.
.build-complete

# OPERATOR SECRETS. The build context is handed to the Docker daemon IN FULL and lands in the layer
# cache, whether or not any COPY names the file. `deploy/testnet-miner/.env` is created by every operator
# who follows the miner README, and it carries the relay token.
#
# ⭐ EXCLUDE THE FAMILY, NOT THE CANONICAL NAME. `*.bak` above matches only what ENDS in `.bak`, so a
# rotation backup called `.env.bak-20260802-034827` walks straight through it. `*.key` does not match
# `.webui_secret_key` either -- it ends in `_key`. Name the shapes, not the examples.
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/*.env.bak*
**/*secret_key*
**/*.secret
**/*.pem
**/*.vrf
# Templates are not secrets and must stay in the context: `**/.env.*` would otherwise take them, and an
# over-broad exclusion that breaks a build quietly is barely better than a too-narrow one.
!**/.env.example
!**/*.env.example
